-- ============================================================================
-- PRODUCTION VARIANT: identical to fix_apartment_productid_naming_scheme.sql
-- except it omits SALES_OPPORTUNITY_PRODUCT, which does not exist in the
-- production schema (confirmed via INFORMATION_SCHEMA.KEY_COLUMN_USAGE on
-- prod - it exists in dev but that migration hasn't reached prod yet, a
-- pre-existing schema-drift issue unrelated to this fix). Dev already ran
-- the full version successfully; this is the prod run only.
-- ============================================================================

-- ============================================================================
-- Rename the 68 apartment products created via the SAWA / Watan House Excel
-- imports (2026-07) from the auto-generated numeric sequence (10028-10095) to
-- the building/floor/unit ID scheme already used by every other project in
-- this database (e.g. project 109's A1-01, A1-02, ... A8-52).
--
-- WHY
-- The import script left ProductId blank and let CreateProduct auto-assign
-- sequence numbers, since that was the only option available at the time.
-- Every pre-existing apartment project instead uses ProductId = "{BUILDING}-
-- {FLOOR_DIGIT}{SEQ_ON_FLOOR}" (e.g. A1-01 = building A1, floor 0, 1st unit;
-- A1-52 = building A1, floor 5, 2nd unit), with ProductName = "Apartment
-- {ProductId}". This script brings the 68 new rows in line with that.
--
-- SCOPE
--   SAWA (project 10339): 48 units, building "Block A" -> shortened to "A"
--     (SAWA's source sheet only ever referred to a single "Block A"; if more
--     blocks are added later they should follow the same A2, A3... pattern
--     project 109 uses).
--   Watan House (project 113): 20 units across buildings B166/B167/B168/B169
--     (already short-form, unchanged). The 4 units with no source data in the
--     original sheet (B166-A/B167-B/B168-C/B169-D) are placed as the next
--     free per-building/floor sequence slot rather than given a floor of
--     their own.
--
-- WHY FOREIGN_KEY_CHECKS=0
-- Every FK in this schema that references PRODUCT.PRODUCT_ID is declared
-- NO ACTION (verified via INFORMATION_SCHEMA.REFERENTIAL_CONSTRAINTS on both
-- dev and prod), so there is no cascade to rely on and no way to update
-- PRODUCT and its ~65 referencing tables in a single consistent order without
-- momentarily breaking referential integrity mid-transaction. Disabling FK
-- checks for the duration of this transaction only (all in the same
-- connection/transaction, checks re-enabled before COMMIT) is the standard
-- way to do a bulk primary-key rename in MySQL/InnoDB.
--
-- WHAT WAS ACTUALLY FOUND REFERENCING THE OLD IDS (both dev and prod, swept
-- across every one of the ~66 FK columns pointing at PRODUCT.PRODUCT_ID):
--   SALES_REQUEST: exactly 2 rows
--     10888 -> PRODUCT_ID 10037 (Apt-102, now A-12) -- SALES_REQUEST_CREATED
--     10889 -> PRODUCT_ID 10039 (Apt-104, now A-14) -- SALES_REQUEST_APPROVED
--   Every other FK column: 0 rows in both databases.
--   PAYMENT has no PRODUCT_ID column at all (only SALES_REQUEST_ID, which
--   this script does not change), so no payment rows need any adjustment.
-- The UPDATE statements below still cover all ~66 FK columns unconditionally
-- (each is a harmless no-op where nothing matches) so this script stays
-- correct even if more sales requests/reservations/etc. get created against
-- these apartments between now and whenever this is actually run.
--
-- NOTE ON PRODUCT 10039 IN PRODUCTION
-- Its ProductName was already manually edited to "A-104" at some point after
-- creation (a partial, not-quite-matching attempt at this same fix -- the
-- real convention is 2-digit floor+seq, not the original 3-digit suffix).
-- This script overwrites it to the fully correct 'Apartment A-14' / ID
-- 'A-14', consistent with every other row.
--
-- PRE-FLIGHT CHECK (run manually before this script, on each target DB):
--   Confirmed 2026-07: none of the 68 new target IDs (A-01..A-58,
--   B166-01..B169-42) currently exist as a PRODUCT_ID anywhere in dev or
--   production. Re-run the collision check below if time has passed since
--   this was written.
--
-- RUN ORDER: run this file whole, once per database (dev first, verify, then
-- production). Not yet run against either database -- review before running.
-- ============================================================================

